Question ------ Table: Employee

+--------------+---------+
| Column Name  | Type    |
+--------------+---------+
| id           | int     |
| name         | varchar |
| salary       | int     |
| departmentId | int     |
+--------------+---------+
id is the primary key (column with unique values) for this table.
departmentId is a foreign key (reference column) of the ID from the Department table.
Each row of this table indicates the ID, name, and salary of an employee. It also contains the ID of their department.
 

Table: Department

+-------------+---------+
| Column Name | Type    |
+-------------+---------+
| id          | int     |
| name        | varchar |
+-------------+---------+
id is the primary key (column with unique values) for this table.
Each row of this table indicates the ID of a department and its name.
 

A company's executives are interested in seeing who earns the most money in each of the company's departments. A high earner in a department is an employee who has a salary in the top three unique salaries for that department.

Write a solution to find the employees who are high earners in each of the departments.

Return the result table in any order.

The result format is in the following example.

 

Example 1:

Input: 
Employee table:
+----+-------+--------+--------------+
| id | name  | salary | departmentId |
+----+-------+--------+--------------+
| 1  | Joe   | 85000  | 1            |
| 2  | Henry | 80000  | 2            |
| 3  | Sam   | 60000  | 2            |
| 4  | Max   | 90000  | 1            |
| 5  | Janet | 69000  | 1            |
| 6  | Randy | 85000  | 1            |
| 7  | Will  | 70000  | 1            |
+----+-------+--------+--------------+
Department table:
+----+-------+
| id | name  |
+----+-------+
| 1  | IT    |
| 2  | Sales |
+----+-------+
Output: 
+------------+----------+--------+
| Department | Employee | Salary |
+------------+----------+--------+
| IT         | Max      | 90000  |
| IT         | Joe      | 85000  |
| IT         | Randy    | 85000  |
| IT         | Will     | 70000  |
| Sales      | Henry    | 80000  |
| Sales      | Sam      | 60000  |
+------------+----------+--------+
Explanation: 
In the IT department:
- Max earns the highest unique salary
- Both Randy and Joe earn the second-highest unique salary
- Will earns the third-highest unique salary

In the Sales department:
- Henry earns the highest salary
- Sam earns the second-highest salary
- There is no third-highest salary as there are only two employees
 

Constraints:

There are no employees with the exact same name, salary and department.









Solution ---------   SELECT 
                     d.name AS Department,
                     e.name AS Employee,
                     e.salary AS Salary
                     FROM
                    (
                    SELECT *,
                    DENSE_RANK() OVER(
                    PARTITION BY departmentId
                    ORDER BY salary DESC
                    ) AS rnk
                   FROM Employee
                    ) e
                   JOIN Department d
                   ON e.departmentId = d.id
                   WHERE e.rnk <= 3;





Explanation ---------  So In This Question We Are Given An Employee Table & Department Table & What The Question Is Asking Is That The Employees Who Are High
                       Earners In Each Department Like IT Department And Sales Department . 

                       We Start With The Inner Query [ select * , dense_rank() over (partition by departmentid order by salary desc ) as rnk From Employee ] 
                       Since The Question Clearly Asked Us High Earners Employees In Each Department So Here We Will Use The Concept Of Dense Rank & Partition
                       By Departmentid Beacuse We Want To Segregate The Employees Department Wise . On The Other Hand , To Get High Earner Employees We Use Dense 
                       Rank Bcz It Will Rank On The Basis Of Their Salary From Highest To Lowest Which Is The Reason Why We Wrote [ Order By Salary Desc ] Since 
                       Its An Inner Query There Are Things Left To Do We Have To Name This Whole Query In Alias So We Give This Query A Nickname Which Is "rnk"
                       And [ From Employee ] Means That We Are Begining With The Employees Table . 

                       Now Since We Need Our Output To Look Like Wherein I Have The Department Name , Employee Name And Salary So We Write Outer Query Which Goes Like
                       [ Select d.name as department e.name as employee e.salary as salary ] So We Take Data From Their Respective Tables Like Department Name From 
                       Department Table , Employment Name From Employee Table & Salary From Employee Table And We Select All This From Table Employee And We Name It As 
                       "e" . 

                      Now We Need To Join Both The Tables Employee And Department Table Bcz If We Don't Do It We Can't Come To Our Final Output Bcz They Are Both Linked 
                      To Each Other So Here We Use The Join Function & We Do It On The Basis Of Department Id Bcz It Is Common In Both The Tables . After Joining These 2
                      Tables We Write A Condition Where e.rnk <=3 Which Means Where The Rank Should Come Uptill Top 3 . We Finally Get The High Earners Employee In Each
                      Department . 

                      In Our Output In IT Department We Get Max As The Highest Earner Employee With 90,000 Then Followed By Joe , Randy , & Will With 85,000 , 85,000 & 70,000
                      Coming In The Sales Department We Get Henry As The Highest Earner Employee With 80,000 Then Sam With 60,000 . 

                      One Important Thing To Notice Here Is That We Used Dense Rank () Bcz We Wanted To Give Ranks Equally To Those Whp Got The Same salary Like As Can See
                      In Our Case With 85,000 Salary , If We Might've Used Row Rank () Concept Here Then It Might've Taken Only One 85,000 So This Was A Crucial Point To 
